    <div class="post-container" id="fpc-2071423">
                      
		<div class="post" id="post-2071423">
		<div class="long">
			<div class="head">
									<div class="options">
						<a href="javascript:;" onclick="togglePostFold(event,2071423)" class="btn btn-default btn-small btn-sm">Fold</a>
					</div>
								<div class="title" id="post-title-2071423">
																		
															</div>
				<div class="info">
					<span class="printuser deleted" data-id="1361261"><img class="small" src="http://www.wikidot.com/common--images/avatars/default/a16.png" alt=""/>(account deleted)</span> <span class="odate time_1405706098 format_%25e%20%25b%20%25Y%2C%20%25H%3A%25M%7Cagohover">18 Jul 2014 17:54</span>
														</div>
			</div>
			<div class="content" id="post-content-2071423">
									

<div class="collapsible-block">
<div class="collapsible-block-folded"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">+&nbsp;line-by-line</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded" style="display:none">
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded-link"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">-&nbsp;Hide&nbsp;text</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-content">
<blockquote>
<p>It was the fall of 2001. In the past year, the country was rearing its ugly head</p>
</blockquote>
<p>I'm assuming that you're referring to the response to 9/11, in which case this makes no sense because the attacks occurred in the fall of 2001. Maybe you mean 2002. Also, this is sort of a strange usage of the phrase &quot;rearing its ugly head&quot;. That usually refers to underlying conditions or temporary circumstances becoming apparent, not necessarily to something that's always there (e.g., &quot;the country&quot;).</p>
<blockquote>
<p>For us teenagers, that meant joining the SWAT's</p>
</blockquote>
<p>I'm not a law enforcement officer, but I'm fairly certain that SWAT teams are composed of experienced and highly-trained operators taken from existing pools of personnel. I'm also fairly certain that one does not get the chance to apply for such an assignment as a teenage yahoo with no prior experience.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>-ready to chew into the cesspit that was the city of gold</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Among the other issues with using highly stylized prose is an obligation to keep the language at least somewhat moored to literary convention, established meanings of words and concepts, and to the story itself. The phrase above doesn't really do any of that. How does one &quot;chew into a cesspit&quot;? And when did New York start being referred to as &quot;the city of gold&quot;?</p>
<blockquote>
<p>I can't remember how many houses we assaulted, how many doors we kicked in, how many people we shot. For some reason, our exploits never even made the news. Every time we clicked on the box it was something about a big pop musician of the day.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>I guess we're at the point now where there are people who aren't old enough to actually remember the early 00s. Because even with all the stuff that went down, civilian police weren't going around regularly carrying out extrajudicial killings in New York (which, by the way, is not something that would be even remotely possible to cover up), and the news media was saturated with &quot;War on Terror&quot; related content, making the stock &quot;the news is only about celebrities&quot; criticism both odd to make here and also completely inaccurate.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>When our trucks rolled up, it suddenly occurred to me that we had diverged into suburbia.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>So a SWAT officer who's engaged in a high-risk mission involving other assets just now realizes where in fact he's supposed to do his thing? What?</p>
<blockquote>
<p>It's no problem to shoot someone who killed your mother with a plane; it's a whole different story when you start shooting tired men with ashen arms.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Your protagonist here sounds less like an overeager youth and more like a psychopath. Psycopaths aren't generally very relatable characters.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>I'm not exactly sure how it happened, but somehow I ended up in a Mexican standoff with my captain and an invisible sniper</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Very awkward transition in the action (&quot;I'm not sure how it happened&quot; is essentially &quot;and then some stuff that I don't really want to bother to describe&quot; here). And how does one engage in a &quot;Mexican standoff&quot; with an invisible sniper? Wouldn't he just shoot you? And how does the protagonist even know there's an &quot;invisible sniper&quot; around? And why is he even there for the purposes of the story, since he apparently doesn't figure into anything?</p>
<blockquote>
<p>Another three seconds later, another brain case was subjected to the act.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>I'm unsure how someone who's just been shot in the head is going to be able to observe something like that.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>Prison came like water to me</p>
</blockquote>
<p>&#8230;what?</p>
<blockquote>
<p>It gave me time to think, to resign myself to my fate of an inexplicable 30 year sentence</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Was the guy not around for his trial or something? The prison sentence may be unjust or political or whatever, but &quot;inexplicable&quot; isn't a term I'd use, given that they have to charge you with something and go through the whole due process thing, especially since your man here doesn't sound like he's in Guantanamo or anything.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>world-wizened</p>
</blockquote>
<p>I think the term you're looking for here is &quot;world weary&quot;</p>
<blockquote>
<p>The people we're fighting have more sway than us in this department.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>&#8230;so then how is the Foundation able to conduct weekly meetings with a person of interest openly, and then spring him without resistance?<br />
_</p>
</div>
</div>
</div>
<p>The piece gets off on the wrong foot with the puzzling characterization of post-9/11 events, and delves into &quot;unskilled teenager who gets recruited to be a supar-badass&quot; narrative, which has never made any sense to me. The writing is strangely florid and hard to understand in a lot of places, and doesn't really read like something an LEO who's spent years in prison would write.</p>
<p>I also have to imagine the Foundation (as at least a somewhat competent organization in my view) is a little more careful in its recruiting practices. I question whether they would have any interest in bringing aboard a guy with a TBI whose only demonstrated skills are shooting things and then sometimes not shooting things if they're not a Middle Easterner of some sort, and I'm fairly certain that they don't go around saying openly that they're with the Foundation and then giving folks the chance to turn them down and go about their business. To say nothing of handing people of uncertain loyalties files emblazoned with an <em>official Foundation logo</em>.</p>
<p>Overall, this doesn't really ring true to me on any level, and the lack of any logical consistency keeps from buying into the story at any point.</p>

							</div>

								
					
